<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peat Bog Soldiers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a> (first performed 28 August <a href="/years/1933">1933</a>)</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Die Moorsoldaten</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;Peat Bog Soldiers&quot; (Die Moorsoldaten) is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s best-known protest songs. It was written, composed and first performed in 1933 by prisoners of the Nazi regime in Börgermoor, one of the Emsland camps near Papenburg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, which held political opponents of the Third Reich; the site is now part of the commune of Surwold, and a documentation and information centre on the camps stands in Papenburg.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1933 the camp KZ Börgermoor held about 1,000 Socialist and Communist internees. They were banned from singing existing political songs, so they wrote and composed their own. The words were written by Johann Esser, a miner, and Wolfgang Langhoff, an actor; the music was composed by Rudi Goguel. It was later adapted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a> and Ernst Busch, and when arranging it for Busch, Eisler made several changes to the rhythm, condensing the meter into two-four time.</p><p>The song was first sung at a Zircus Konzentrani, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> circus whose name played on the Circus Sarrasani. In Goguel's account, sixteen singers, mostly members of the Solinger workers choir, marched in with spades over their shoulders while he conducted with the handle of a broken spade. By the end of the second verse nearly all of the thousands of prisoners present joined the chorus, and by the end the SS guards were also singing. At the last line the singers rammed their spades into the ground and marched out, leaving them standing in the bog like crosses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Langhoff and Esser's original song runs to six verses with refrains. It describes heath and bog stretching as far as the eye can see, a camp built behind <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbed-wire">barbed wire</a>, columns marching out each morning to dig under the sun, and prisoners longing for their parents, wives and children while guards pace up and down. The last verse refuses complaint, since winter cannot last for ever, and the final refrain looks to a day when the peat bog soldiers will no longer march with their spades to the moor. For performance, and so for most translations, verses two, three and four are usually left out. The melody is slow and simple, like a soldier's march, and deliberately repetitive, echoing the daily grind of hard labour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The song was popular with German refugees in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the 1930s and was used as a marching song by the German volunteers of the International Brigades during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, where it became a Republican anthem. Other nationalities soon took it up, and it appears in almost all the anthologies of Spanish Civil War songs. It became a symbol of resistance during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and is sung by the peace movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a> recorded it on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Free Men, and later versions came from the Chad Mitchell Trio in 1965, The Dubliners in 1970, Swan Arcade in 1973 and Lankum in 2017.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peat Bog Soldiers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
